\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Market Debt Weaker Vs Treasurys Even After Mubarak Exit\b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2011\par 21:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Anjali Cordeiro \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Emerging-market debt was weaker versus Treasurys Friday, after Egyptian President Hosni Mubarak resigned from office.\f28 \par \par \uc2 While Mubarak's departure was seen as a generally positive development for emerging-market assets, the inflationary pressures that have sprung up in the developing world have remained a key concern for investors. The worries about inflation have held down emerging-market assets in recent days and have generally overshadowed even positive developments in Egypt.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the risk premium, or spread, on the \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  Emerging Markets Bond Index Global was 6 basis points wider at 282 basis points over Treasurys. In price terms the index was off 0.23%.\par \par \uc2 While many emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds stayed range bound, the South African rand and Turkish lira gained ground against the dollar immediately after news of Mubarak's departure. The Egyptian pound initially weakened against the dollar and then later pared those losses and went on to strengthen against the dollar. The dollar was recently off 0.45% at EGP5.8721 from the previous close. Egypt's spread on the Embig was 5 basis points wider at 304 basis points over Treasurys. The index was up 0.02% in price terms.\par \par \uc2 Mubarak's departure, while a small positive for emerging markets, is unlikely to drive any "big directional changes" in these assets, said John Peta, an emerging-market portfolio manager at Acadian Asset Management. Emerging-market investors are at the moment more focused on global inflation, he said, and particularly on the monetary policy moves of central banks in the developing world.\par \par \uc2 News out of Asia highlighted the pressures that emerging nations face in managing inflationary pressures and the value of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Vietnam devalued the dong Friday for the fourth time in 14 months, conceding to a lack of market confidence in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it struggles with a gaping trade deficit. The move is risky, as it could stoke already-strong inflationary pressures and make it more expensive for the country to service its dollar-denominated debt. The \uc2 State Bank of Vietnam\uc2  set its exchange rate for the dollar at VND20,693, up 9.3% from VND18,932 Thursday. Vietnam was 3 basis points tighter on the Embig to 373 basis points over Treasurys. In price terms the index was up 0.38%.\par \par \uc2 News of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets continued to trickle in. The Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on Friday to buy $130 million at an average rate of 2.7658 soles to the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, in Latin America, Mexico's industrial production grew more than expected in December, with manufacturing, mining and construction output all rising from the same month a year ago, bringing full-year 2010 growth to 6.1%, the National Statistics Institute, or Inegi, said Friday. Mexico's spread was 2 basis points wider at 155 basis points over Treasurys. In price terms the index was flat. The peso strengthened against the dollar, with the dollar recently down 0.34% at MXN12.0486.\par \par \uc2 -By Anjali Cordeiro, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2200; anjali.cordeiro@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Nguyen Pham Muoi and Anthony Harrup contributed to this article. [ 02-11-11 1551ET ]\par \par \uc2 1810 4071   \par \par