\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Peru Central Bank Intervenes \highlight\uc2  For 1st Time In 2011\b0\par\par\uc2 1,314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2011\par 20:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Robert Flint, 212-416-2216; \par robert.flint@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1903 GMT [Dow Jones] The Central Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Peru intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to buy $18 million at an average USD/PEN rate of 2.766. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchased dollars regularly last year to smooth out volatility, but Tuesday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was the first since October. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Peru\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's sol has been on an appreciating trend recently due in part to strong inflows of capital. Last year, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought a total of $8.96 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par (sophie.kevany@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1855 GMT [Dow Jones] CAD has trimmed its gains modestly as Canadian Finance Minister Jim Flaherty holds a news conference in Ottawa. Flaherty said the economic recovery in Canada remains fragile and that there's "resistance" to the unemployment rate coming down. USD/CAD has recovered to the 0.9920 area in the wake of the Flaherty headlines from the 0.9903 area just before. It's still down sharply from its late Monday level of 1.0014. There's some sensitivity in the market to any discussion of the unemployment rate ahead of the January jobs data on Friday. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1842 GMT [Dow Jones] Bonds backing \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPTI Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  plunged as the Canadian oil-sands company said it has retained Lazard to assist in its ongoing review of strategic alternatives. \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPTI Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2 's 8.25% notes due 2014 were down 5.25 points to 54.75 cents on the dollar Tuesday afternoon and its 7.875% notes due 2014 were down 5.75 to 54.75, both in heavy trade, according to MarketAxess. Both issues are now down nearly 10 cents in the past week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (michael.aneiro@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1820 GMT [Dow Jones] A true test of the muni market's recent pricing stability may not come for another few weeks when more bond sales start happening. At $12.2 bln, January was the lowest monthly sale tally for munis in more than a decade, according to Thomson Reuters. "We expect municipal new issue volume to continue to be below average for at least the next several weeks," says RBC Capital Market's Chris Mauro, citing the Bond Buyer's $8.5B in 30-day visible supply index. "The price stability of the last week or so will be challenged" if and when volume picks up, says Janney's Alan Schankel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (kelly.nolan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1806 GMT [Dow Jones]GECC's $717.275M bond, offered Monday via GE Equipment Small Ticket LLC, has launched. The bond is backed by equipment loans and leases and is being sold via the private Rule 144a market. The deal is scheduled to price this week. The largest tranche of the six-part deal is valued at $245 million and has launched at 36 basis points over Eurodollar synthetic forward, or EDSF, a benchmark. Price guidance on this 1.25 year tranche was in the range of 35 to 40 bps. Joint lead managers are \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  and BofAML.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1729 GMT [Dow Jones] Mexico's overseas worker remittances reached $21.2 billion in 2010, virtually unchanged from 2009--signaling that Mexico doesn't depend on hot money flows to finance the current account gap (unlike Brazil), says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "That, along with relatively high interest rates (4.5%) and official ambivalence with a stronger peso leads us to maintain our bullish stance on MXN," it added. Plus, with projected GDP growth of 3.9% and headline inflation of 3.9% for 2011, rate hikes could happen sooner than the expected 2012--another boost for MXN. "The improved growth outlook for both Mexico and the US as well as building price pressures are likely to push tightening into 2011. That would be peso-supportive if and when market expectations shift that way," it added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1726 GMT [Dow Jones] A new commercial mortgage-backed security is on offer: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  have a $1.548 bln bond. The first CMBS of the year, a $2.2 bln deal, was offered by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  earlier this week. The 1.548 bln security is backed by 37 loans on 79 properties. The top property types include retail at 43.6%, office at 28%, hospitality at 9.8% and industrial at 7.7%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1718 GMT [Dow Jones] The latest manufacturing PMI readings out of emerging Asian nations further support the case for imminent interest-rate hikes, said Capital Economics. "Manufacturing in Asia looks set to gain more momentum in coming months at a time when inflation pressures are on the rise," they said. Taiwan and India each saw strong output and new orders in January. The economists expect Taiwanese authorities to hike up the policy rate again, by 12.5 basis points at their next meeting in March. The repo rate in India should reach 7% by mid-2011, they said. The South Korean economy also continues to expand, with a PMI reading above 50. Capital Economics forecasts its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to lift its base rate by 25 basis points. (cynthia.lin@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York. 1-212-416-3100 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1702 GMT [Dow Jones] Monetary policy tightening in Mexico is unlikely this year, even though the inflation outlook warrants it, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  writes. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  projects both headline and core inflation to bottom out year-on-year at the end of Q1 and to end the year at 4.25% and 3.75%, respectively. "It is ... unclear whether the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would feel compelled to raise rates in a scenario like the one we expect," it said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1635 GMT [Dow Jones] March bunds hit contract lows as improving risk sentiment helps drive equities higher and pushes peripheral yields lower, in turn damping the appeal of safe haven government bonds. The extra yield demanded by investors to buy Spanish bonds instead of bunds eased to its tightest level since November while stocks held on to the day's gains after the better than expected US ISM January manufacturing report. The ADP employment report is the key economic release Wednesday, while Portugal sells up to EUR1.25 billion of T-bills. March bunds are down 0.73 at 123.01, having hit a new contract low of 122.88. The 10-year bund yield climbs 7bps to 3.222%, the highest since February. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1628 GMT [Dow Jones] US manufacturing opened 2011 with its greatest strength in nearly seven years, helping propel an economy struggling against a weak job market and a sputtering housing sector. The data is important because it helps shape investors' perception of when the Fed will hike US rates, an crucial factor for the dollar's appeal. ISM's manufacturing report was better than expected and the highest since May 2004. Manufacturing has led the economy's slow recovery from the recession. Government data last week showed gross domestic product grew at a faster pace in the fourth quarter as consumers spent more. But high joblessness remains, restraining the recovery. Separate data Tuesday said spending on construction projects in the US tumbled by 2.5% in December.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (robert.flint@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1617 GMT [Dow Jones] The Treasury Department is set to release its latest quarterly refunding details at 1400 GMT (9 a.m. EST) Wednesday. Many economists forecast a package of $72 bln for the refunding, unchanged from the previous quarterly refunding in November. The sales due next week will include $32 bln in three-year notes, $24 bln in 10-year notes and $16 bln in 30-year bonds. (min.zeng@dowjones.com) [ 02-01-11 1403ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110201e721000d5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
