\par\par \b \uc2 Chile, Colombia, Peru Finance Ministers Meet To Discuss  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2010\par 23:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--The Finance Ministers of Chile, Colombia and Peru, along with Nobel-prize winning economist Joseph Stiglitz, will discuss the recent appreciation of their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar, the ministers said Friday.\par \par \uc2 The three countries' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have appreciated as the region rapidly recovers from last year's global financial crisis, while industrialized nations such as the U.S. and Europe struggle to get back on their feet.\par \par \uc2 Slow growth in the industrialized nations, and their expansive monetary policies, have helped fuel the strength of the three nations' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Chile's Finance Minister Felipe Larrain told reporters.\par \par \uc2 "We're going to study the global economic scenario, particularly the situation in industrialized economies," said Larrain. "We're concerned about this because our countries, which are part of the global economy...and despite the strengths we've developed, aren't immune to what's happening abroad."\par \par \uc2 Chile is one of the remaining countries in the region, however, that hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, despite the peso nearing 31-month highs and exporters' demands for a lifeline as the peso's strength hurts the competitiveness of their products.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has maintained that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a tool that's always available, but it will only use it if the exchange rate, in real terms, moves out of line with its long-term fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 The three nations want to compare how they are responding to the challenges presented by their appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and see what options they have.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  has maintained a policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to smooth out movements of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although it hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to purchase U.S. dollars since Oct. 13.\par \par \uc2 "We want to see if we can eventually take mutual measures to confront this uncertain situation brought about by developed nations, that we'll be dealing with for a few years to come..what's important is that we share our experiences, because the problem is shared," Peru's Finance Minister Ismael Benavides said.\par \par \uc2 For its part, Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buying $20 million daily in the spot market in a bid to curb the peso's strength.\par \par \uc2 "There are exchange-rate challenges, and every day we have to react to what's happening. In the meeting we'll discuss what's worked and what hasn't," Colombia's Finance Minister Juan Carlos Echeverry said.\par \par \uc2 Chile's Larrain added "with humility we can say that if Latin America was the source of problems in the past, today the situation has changed, it is a source of solutions for the world and in that sense we're happy to be working together."\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 10-12-10 2219GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101210e6ca001h8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
