\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Peru's Sol Is Expected To Continue To Appreciate\b0\par\par\uc2 198 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2010\par 20:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's sol is expected to continue to appreciate, in part due to the nation's strong economic fundamentals, Banco Santander's chief economist for Latin America said.\par \par \uc2 "The most logical movement of the exchange rate will be a sustained appreciation of the sol in respect to three years ago or five years ago," Jose Juan Ruiz said Wednesday in a meeting with reporters.\par \par \uc2 He pointed to Peru's expected growth in gross domestic product of about 8.5% this year and 6.0% next year.\par \par \uc2 The free-floating sol has appreciated by 2.73% against the U.S. dollar in the last 12-month period.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to smooth out movements of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also worked well, Ruiz said.\par \par \uc2 "Peru doesn't have any problem with its exchange rate. The authorities aren't letting any temporary volatility get out of control," he added.\par \par \uc2 -By Robert Kozak, Dow Jones Newswires; 51-1-99927 7269; peru@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 24, 2010 14:41 ET (19:41 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101124e6bo004rl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
