\par\par \b \uc2 PREVIEW-Peru  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  seen holding rates steady\b0\par\par\uc2 257 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2010\par 17:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 WHAT: Peru's interest rate decision\par \par \uc2 WHEN: Thursday, Nov. 11, around 8 p.m. EST\par \par \uc2 OUTCOME: No change in rate seen\par \par \uc2 REUTERS FORECAST: Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to hold its benchmark interest rate <PECPI=ECI> steady at 3 percent on Thursday for the second straight month as inflation is seen well within its target.\par \par \uc2 It unexpectedly held rates steady in October after five straight increases.\par \par \uc2 Peru is one of the world's fastest-growing economies, expected to expand about 8 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 But inflation is relatively benign. This year prices are forecast to rise less than 3 percent, the ceiling of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range. The low end of the range is 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Consumer prices dipped in September and October by 0.03 percent and 0.14 percent, respectively, amid cheaper food and beverage prices.\par \par \uc2 MARKET IMPACT: Seen limited as a flood of foreign capital into Peru has pushed the sol <PEN=PE> to a two-year high of 2.80 per dollar. Some observers have said higher local rates could make it even more attractive at a time of very low yields in the United States.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this year to soak up excess liquidity, buying billions to temper volatility, which could lead to inflation if the sol were to suddenly reverse direction after excessive strengthening.\par \par \uc2 PERU