\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Peru's Foreign Reserves Rise To Record High $40.6B\b0\par\par\uc2 182 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2010\par 16:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's net international reserves rose to a record high $40.63 billion as of August 17, boosted in part by heavy U.S. dollar buying by the Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly since July 18 to purchase dollars, as strong foreign capital inflows increase appreciation pressures on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sol.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said late Friday that net international reserves rose $2.0 billion from the end of July.\par \par \uc2 The increase is also tied to increased deposits in the financial system, it added.\par \par \uc2 At the end of December last year, the net international reserves stood at $33.14 billion.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  position on July 13 was $29.18 billion.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  position doesn't include public sector deposits with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or commercial bank reserves.\par \par \uc2 -Robert Kozak, Dow Jones Newswires; 511-99927-7269; peru@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 23, 2010 10:14 ET (14:14 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100823e68n003i6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
