\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Peru's Foreign Reserves Rise To Record High $37.11 Bln\b0\par\par\uc2 204 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 2010\par 15:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's net international reserves rose to a record $37.11 billion as of July 13, boosted in part by heavy U.S. dollar buying by the Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly since July 18 to purchase dollars, as strong foreign capital inflows increase appreciation pressures on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sol.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said late Friday that net international reserves are the equivalent of 28% of gross domestic product, and are more than the nation's total private and public external debt.\par \par \uc2 The latest total is $3.97 billion higher than at the end of 2009, and $1.77 billion higher than at the end of June.\par \par \uc2 The increase is also tied to increased deposits in the financial system, it added.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  position on July 13 was $26.64 billion.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  position doesn't include public sector deposits with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or commercial bank reserves.\par \par \uc2 -Robert Kozak, Dow Jones Newswires;511-99927-7269; peru@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 18, 2010 09:36 ET (13:36 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100718e67i0026v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex1