\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Peru \highlight\uc2 's Ctrl Bk  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  A Signal - Bank\b0\par\par\uc2 911 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2010\par 14:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1341 GMT [Dow Jones] The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Central Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Peru\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy about $1.4 billion recently are a signal that it doesn't want a significant appreciation of the sol, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  said. "We expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to continue \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market if capital inflows remain high; with the economy still weak and inflation low, we do not think that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is concerned about the expansion of the monetary aggregates," it said. (RSK)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1350 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds move higher after US weekly jobs claims increased unexpectedly by 36K versus a drop of 4K forecast. The March bund futures contract is currently up 0.22 on the day at 122.83, from 122.67 before the data and the 10-year benchmark bund is down 0.20 at 100.04, yielding 3.246%, from 3.269%. (NKA)\par \par \uc2 1347 GMT [Dow Jones] Long-term Tsy futures trim early price slide on surprising jump in weekly jobless claims. Mar 30-yr Tsy bonds test overnight high of 118-04, which matches Wed's one-month high. Recently up 1/32 at 118-00. Mar 10-yr Tsy notes remain inside Wed's range. Recently 2/32 lower at 117-11. (HLP)\par \par \uc2 1344 GMT [Dow Jones] In the Labor Department's Thursday report, the number of continuing claims--those drawn by workers for more than one week in the week ended Jan. 9--fell by 18,000 to 4,599,000 from the preceding week's revised level of 4,617,000. But the broader unadjusted tally of claims covering state, extended and emergency benefits programs jumped to 11.9 million in the Jan 2 week, up more than a million from the prior week's total. (KGM)\par \par \uc2 1344 GMT [Dow Jones] Poland's below-consensus December industrial output data suggests that restocking by companies is progressing but slowly, \uc2 Bank BPH\uc2  says. Nonetheless, industrial production in the fourth quarter rose by some 5% on year, BPH estimates. The latter supports view that the economy is accelerating and the forecast of 2.7% annual GDP growth in the fourth quarter, and 1.6% growth in the whole year. (MES)\par \par \uc2 1342 GMT [Dow Jones] Eurodollar futures prices stay mostly negative, but bounce from the lows on unexpected rise in weekly jobless claim filings. Quarterly 2010 contracts recently unchanged to down 1 BP. 2011 Eurodollars down as much as 1.5 BP. Aug 2010 fed-funds futures price in 94% chance for FOMC to boost funds rate to 0.5% at Aug. 10 meeting. That's unchanced from Wed. (HLP)\par \par \uc2 1340 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasury prices are paring more losses as investors digest the jobless claims report. Two-year yields 0.9% while the 10-year note is down 1/32 to yield 3.67%. The 30-year bond is 1/32 lower to yield 4.55%.(MOZ)\par \par \uc2 1339 GMT [Dow Jones] The cost to protect bank bonds is down sharply across the board following Goldman's blowout 4Q profit of $4.95 billion - which beat analysts expectations. During the same period 2009, the bank posted a loss of $2.29 billion. Goldman CDS quoted 88/93, down by 10 bps. That means it costs $88,000 to protect $10 million of sr bonds against default for 5-yrs. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  at 117/122, better by 3 bps; BAC at 102/107 improved by 5 bps. \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  has showed the greatest gain -at 167/172 bps- better by 13 bps so far Thursday. (KAG)\par \par \uc2 1337 GMT [Dow Jones] Not only did an economist at the U.S. Labor Department attribute last week's jump in new claims in part because the Christmas and New Year's holidays created a backlog in some states, he added that a total of seven states from Thursday's data had estimated claims figures because of the Martin Luther King, Jr. holiday. As for the jump, he said, "It is not an economic thing - it is an administrative thing." (KGM)\par \par \uc2 1334 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets showed little immediate reaction to worse-than-expected US weekly jobless claims -- mainly due to holiday backlog. EUR/USD was 1.4072 from 1.4102 last Wed, while USD/JPY was at 91.76 from 91.23. (FAS)\par \par \uc2 1333 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasury prices lower but off session lows after the release of the latest weekly jobless claims data. The new claims unexpectedly rose partly due to an administrative backlog in processing claims. Continuing claims declined. The two-year note is down 1/32 to yield 0.9% while the 10-year note is down 4/32 to yield 3.68%. The 30-year bond is 6/32 lower to yield 4.55%.(MOZ)\par \par \uc2 1332 GMT [Dow Jones] Initial claims for jobless benefits rose by 36,000 to 482,000 in the week ended Jan. 16. Economists expected a decrease of 4,000. The four-week moving average, which aims to smooth volatility in the data, increased by 7,000 to 448,250 from the previous week's revised average of 441,250. An economist at the U.S. Labor Department said last week's numbers were higher then expected in part because the Christmas and New Year's holidays created a backlog in some states.(KGM) [ 01-21-10 0856ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100121e61l0009p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
