\par\par \b \uc2 Peruvian Sol's Winning Streak Against Dollar Should Continue\b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert Kozak   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2010\par 20:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's sol is on a winning streak and those gains against the greenback are expected to keep going.\par \par \uc2 The sol has appreciated 1.1% in the early weeks of 2010, and by 9.4% over the last 12 months.\par \par \uc2 Economists point to strong economic fundamentals, including a positive trade balance and robust investment levels.\par \par \uc2 "There has been a strong appetite for emerging markets and in particular for the sol, due to expectations of capital inflows," said Roberto Flores, an economist with brokerage Inteligo SAB.\par \par \uc2 "There is a long list of projects that will get under way in Peru this year," he said.\par \par \uc2 Strong demand for the sol has led the Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  every day for the past week, buying about $830 million in its stated goal of easing movements in the market.\par \par \uc2 "The fundamentals of the Peruvian economy, as well as a scenario of a global weakening of the dollar, will add to the strengthening trend of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the coming months, despite \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Banco de Credito said.\par \par \uc2 Economists point to Peru's investment-grade rating from all the major agencies, and rising demand for its exports, especially minerals.\par \par \uc2 Peru's exports are expected to rise to $30 billion this year from about $26 billion last year, Trade Minister Martin Perez said at a press conference this week.\par \par \uc2 After only tepid growth for most of last year, Peru's gross domestic product is starting to show signs of a recovery. GDP in November expanded by a greater-than-expected 4.2%, in part due to a government stimulus program.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecasts growth of 5.5% in GDP this year after expected full-year 2009 growth of about 1%.\par \par \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  said that private domestic investment will easily increase by more than 6% in 2010, adding that public sector investments are also seen posting a "stellar" year.\par \par \uc2 "We believe fundamentals continue to suggest that the sol should appreciate in the months ahead given that private flows will be more than enough to finance this year's current account deficit, and therefore we hold our short USD/PEN we opened last year. We do expect further \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but only to moderate the appreciation rate, rather than to modify the trend," \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Financial consultant Macroconsult forecasts trade and current account surpluses this year, "which leads us to expect that the (appreciating) evolution in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will be seen throughout the year."\par \par \uc2 -By Robert Kozak, Dow Jones Newswires; 511-99927 7269; peru@dowjones.com [ 15-01-10 1919GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100115e61f001b3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
