\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Peru's  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  likely to extend gains in 2010\b0\par\par\uc2 528 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 2009\par 23:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Sol seen ending 2010 at 2.6 to 2.7 per U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 * To strengthen on economic rebound; appetite for risk\par \par \uc2 * Pre-election jitters expected to limit gains\par \par \uc2 By Ursula Scollo\par \par \uc2 LIMA, Dec 9 (Reuters) - Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to strengthen against the U.S. dollar in 2010 as the global and local economies rebound and investors' appetite for risk returns, though the run-up to presidential elections could limit gains.\par \par \uc2 The sol <PEN=PE>, which is currently hovering at around 2.86 per dollar, is expected to close next year at between 2.60 and 2.70, according to analysts consulted by Reuters. That would be a rally of as much as 10 percent in 2010.\par \par \uc2 The sol has gained nearly 9.0 percent this year, trailing gains in most other Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which have risen as investors began to bet again on emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 "The sol may well strengthen next year by about the same amount it did in 2009," said Gonzalo Navarro, head \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Banco Santander in Lima.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, buying and selling dollars to smooth out volatility, and is likely to keep doing so in 2010, though it has said it will follow the tendency of the markets.\par \par \uc2 So far this year, the bank has bought some $910 million to keep the sol from strengthening too fast.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is doing a lot so that the dollar does not weaken further. If the bank had not done anything, I think the sol would be under 2.50," or some 14 percent stronger, said Ricardo Lago, an economist who frequently writes about Peru.\par \par \uc2 2011 ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 Though most analysts expect Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen next year, they say the gains will be softened by uncertainties surrounding the 2011 presidential elections. Candidates are already jockeying for position in the race, and electioneering could pick up in the middle of next year.\par \par \uc2 Peru's economy, which is forecast to grow just 1.0 percent this year, is expected to expand by roughly 5.0 percent in 2010 as prices for most of the country's metals exports recover.\par \par \uc2 "There is a consensus next year will be better for Peru in terms of economics, but it's also a preelection year," said Navarro, with Banco Santander.\par \par \uc2 In the past, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has tended to weaken ahead of elections as investors weigh possible outcomes.\par \par \uc2 Peru's President Alan Garcia, who has pushed free-trade and foreign investment, cannot run in the next election and his ruling APRA party has not yet identified a clear candidate.\par \par \uc2 Lima's Mayor Luis Castaneda and Keiko Fujimori, the daughter of the former President Alberto Fujimori, are currently leading the pack of potential contenders.\par \par \uc2 Neither would likely make major changes to Garcia's economic model, but in third place is Ollanta Humala, an ultra-nationalist, who spooked investors when he nearly won the last presidential election in 2006. (Reporting by Ursula Scollo; Writing by Dana Ford)\par \par \uc2 PERU-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 / (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091209e5c9001g8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
