\par\par \b \uc2 Reuters Summit-UPDATE 2-Peru central banker sees aggressive cut\b0\par\par\uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2009\par 21:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For other news from the Reuters Latin American Investment Summit, click on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.reuters.com/summit/LatinAmericanInvestment09?PID=500"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.reuters.com/summit/LatinAmericanInvestment09?PID=500}}}\uc2 ) (Adds size of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Teresa Cespedes and Terry Wade\par \par \uc2 LIMA, May 4 (Reuters) - Peruvian interest rates will likely be cut this week if low inflation allows the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to implement an "aggressive policy" of reductions, Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Julio Velarde said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will meet on Thursday and may trim its benchmark rate, which is now at 5 percent after three months of cuts to help weather the global economic downturn.\par \par \uc2 "We will continue with an aggressive policy. Aggressive is 50 basis points or more, if inflation keeps falling," Velarde said at the Reuters Latin American Investment Summit in Lima.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has an annual inflation goal of 2 percent, plus or minus a tolerance band of one percentage point. In the last few months, price increases have slowed sharply.\par \par \uc2 "We're within the inflationary range," Velarde said. "We don't see inflationary pressures in the short term. This allows us more space for rate reductions, especially in a context in which the Peruvian sol is gaining against the dollar."\par \par \uc2 The sol <PEN=PE> gained to 2.95 per dollar on Monday, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time since December. It bought only $2 million, but bigger purchases could be in store.\par \par \uc2 "It was simply to send signals that the sol wouldn't move sharply," he said. The sol has rallied since March when it was trading at 3.24 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 IMF AND \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTIONS\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Velarde said \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  is "calmly" evaluating whether to tap a new credit line that the International Monetary Fund has created for high-quality emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Mexico has already asked to borrow from the IMF, and some analysts speculated Peru might be next after Colombia requested $10.4 billion from the multilateral lender.\par \par \uc2 "We are sure that if we ask for it (IMF help) that we'd get it," Velarde said.\par \par \uc2 But Velarde said cash from the IMF might not be needed as Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could buy U.S. dollars to build up its reserves further.\par \par \uc2 "One scenario that we are thinking about, which we haven't ruled out, is that we start to buy dollars," he said.\par \par \uc2 "If we start to buy dollars, because the tendency is for the sol to keep on strengthening, maybe it won't be necessary to have this credit line (from the IMF)," Velarde said.\par \par \uc2 Peru's foreign reserves have more than doubled in the past 32 months to $31 billion, and they would hit new records if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to step up \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Unfortunately other countries in the region didn't build up ammunition in the good times to face tough times," he said.\par \par \uc2 GROWTH\par \par \uc2 Even though economic data has been weak for the past two months, Velarde said growth this year should come in at around 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 That would be less than last year's pace of nearly 10 percent but still one of the fastest rates in the world. Velarde said the economy appears to have grown a bit less than 3 percent in the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 He said exporters are reporting that they are receiving solid orders. Some mining projects that were put on hold have been given the green light thanks to better copper prices, and that the credit system is working well.\par \par \uc2 As a result of an improved outlook, especially for foreign investment, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has narrowed its view for the current account deficit in 2009 to 2.7 percent of GDP from 3.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 "The signs we are seeing are for a trade balance that is increasingly positive and capital inflows that are maybe higher than we estimated," Velarde said. (For summit blog: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.reuters.com/summits/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.reuters.com/summits/}}}\uc2 ) (Writing by Terry Wade; Editing by Diane Craft)\par \par \uc2 LATAM-SUMMIT/PERU (UPDATE 2, PIX, TV)|LANGEN|ABN|D|E|U   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090504e554001e4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
